# taxonomy.kb — the joins that make the hierarchy walkable.
#
# A taxonomy is only as good as its links, and 839 of this corpus's 1,269 kinds had no parent at
# all. Most of those are roots and belong that way — `process`, `activity`, `phenomenon` sit at the
# top and nothing is above them. But 124 were **compound nouns whose head is itself a classified
# kind**, sitting unattached: `island country` had no parent, so Japan was not a country by any
# walk; `precious metal` had none, so gold was not a metal; `blood cell` had none, so a red blood
# cell was not a cell.
#
# That is invisible until something walks the hierarchy. It made `constraint` refuse "what is the
# capital of the country whose currency is the yen" — the search for a country found nothing,
# because no country it knew about was one. Every inheritance, every commonality and every
# odd-one-out was quietly weaker for the same reason.
#
# The rule these were found by is mechanical and stated so it can be checked: an English head-final
# compound **is a** its head. An "island country" is a country; a "chemical element" is an element.
# It does not hold for head-initial phrases — a "creature in mythology" is not a mythology, a "unit
# of time" is not a time — so anything containing " of " or " in " was excluded, and a further list
# was rejected by hand where the head is a different sense of the word ("gas giant" against the
# giant of folklore) or where the phrase is a description rather than a compound.
#
# Nothing here is a new claim about the world. Every line says a thing is what its own name already
# says it is, and the corpus simply never wrote it down.

aerodynamic force | is_a=force
